Here are the types for a singly linked list of `int` values:

```c
// A single node in a linked list
struct ListNode {
  int data; // the data in this node
  struct ListNode *next; // pointer to the next node
};

// Refer to a linked list with a pointer to the first node
typedef struct ListNode *LinkedList;
```

The "next" pointer in the last node of a list should be null.

Write a function called `deleteLast` with a parameter of type `LinkedList`. The function should delete the **last** node of the linked list. Its return value should be the number that was inside the node that was deleted. The function must also free the heap space used for the node being deleted, to prevent a memory leak.

If the list is empty or has only one element, your function should do nothing and return 0. (The caller's pointer to the first node cannot be changed by this function, which is why a one-element list is left alone.)

The header for your function should be:

```c
int deleteLast(LinkedList list)
```

Example code using `deleteLast` (with `addToEnd` and `printList` helpers like those from Assignment 5):

```c
LinkedList list = NULL;
list = addToEnd(list, 7);
list = addToEnd(list, 3);
list = addToEnd(list, 9);
list = addToEnd(list, 4);
list = addToEnd(list, 8);
printf("full list: "); printList(list);

for (int i = 0; i < 5; i++) {
  int val = deleteLast(list);
  printf("deleted %d, list is ", val); printList(list);
}
```

Output:

```
full list: [7,3,9,4,8]
deleted 8, list is [7,3,9,4]
deleted 4, list is [7,3,9]
deleted 9, list is [7,3]
deleted 3, list is [7]
deleted 0, list is [7]
```

questions.answer:
```c
int deleteLast(LinkedList list) {
  if (list == NULL || list->next == NULL) {
    return 0;                      // empty or one-element list
  }
  struct ListNode *prev = list;    // will stop at the second-last node
  while (prev->next->next != NULL) {
    prev = prev->next;
  }
  struct ListNode *last = prev->next;
  int value = last->data;
  prev->next = NULL;               // second-last node becomes the last node
  free(last);
  return value;
}
```

Checked with the example above plus an empty and a one-element list; valgrind reports no leaks or errors.
